bianbu-agent-securityd for Debian/Ubuntu
========================================

This package installs the Bianbu AgentOS security daemon and its D-Bus
contract. The reference Runtime service is not installed by this package;
the Runtime package owns that service.

This release is a development/demo delivery. The Debian package installs a
systemd drop-in that overrides the production unit with:

    BIANBU_SECURITY_CODESIGN=off

No code-signing public key is required, no key package is built, and the
daemon starts normally without a trust root. Agent manifests and
Agent/Runtime/Hook/Tool/Skill code packages do not require signature files or
public keys in this mode. Manifest structure, capability checks, Runtime
identity/lease/digest checks, DLP, audit and sandbox functionality remain
enabled.

The implementation is retained. The base service unit still declares the
product setting:

    BIANBU_SECURITY_CODESIGN=enforce

and securityd continues to support trusted keys and revocation under:

    /etc/bianbu/codesign-keys/

For a product release, remove the packaged
`10-development-demo.conf` drop-in, provision an approved Ed25519 public key,
and run the existing strict release and commercial acceptance gates. Never
ship the private signing key to a target device.

The WebUI unit is enabled and started automatically after installation, and is
enabled for subsequent boots.
This development/demo package also installs a WebUI systemd drop-in that sets:

    BIANBU_WEBUI_DEMO=1

The demo WebUI therefore starts without `BIANBU_WEBUI_TOKEN` and does not
enforce Bearer authentication.  The WebUI authentication, roles and CSRF
implementation are still present in the package.  The product package must
omit `bianbu-security-webui.service.d/10-development-demo.conf`; without that
drop-in, `/etc/bianbu/webui.env` must contain a high-entropy production token
before the service can start.

The demo drop-in removes inherited admin/viewer token variables while retaining
other `/etc/bianbu/webui.env` settings (port, TLS and shell policy).  Demo
uses the unit defaults (`127.0.0.1:8787`) when those settings are absent;
configure the environment file for a product build.

Configure `/etc/bianbu/webui.env` for production before enabling the product
unit.  Even in demo mode, non-loopback WebUI listeners still require TLS (or
the explicit insecure-remote test switch).

The current build defaults to demo mode.  To build a product package, set
`DEB_DEMO=0` when invoking `dpkg-buildpackage`; both development drop-ins are
then omitted, so the base units and the production WebUI token policy apply.
